    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The results presented in this paper are part of a larger study, in which the goal is to constrain the entire geometry of faults accurately interpreted from 3D seismic data at certain levels, but poorly resolved at other levels. The methodology is applied to a set of faults in the Prudhoe Bay oil field on the North Slope of Alaska that are well resolved on shallow horizons, but poorly imaged with increasing depth. Numerical models are used to calculate the displacement fields due to the imposed displacement discontinuity (slip) boundary conditions in the interpreted faults. The computed displacement fields are subtracted from the well-constraining horizons to produce restored \"pre-faulting\" horizons. This procedure is repeated for different postulated fault geometries. The resultant structure contour maps are compared to determine which fault geometry provides structure contours that are most consistent with a priori information about the geologic and tectonic history of the basin.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
